---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="06-string-algos"
  lessonSlug="00-example-count-vowels"
  hook="The textbook's canonical counter pattern (TP §8.7), applied to vowels instead of a single letter."
>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def count_vowels(text):
    count = 0
    vowels = "aeiou"
    for char in text.lower():
        if char in vowels:
            count += 1
    return count

for text in ["banana", "Hello World", "AEIOU", "xyz", ""]:
    print(f"{text!r:>15}: {count_vowels(text)} vowels")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="Source: Think Python §8.7">
    <p>
      <em>"The variable count is initialized to 0 and then incremented each
      time an a is found. When the loop exits, count contains the result."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The three-piece pattern</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Initialize</strong> — <code>count = 0</code></li>
    <li><strong>Traverse + condition</strong> — <code>for char in text: if char in vowels:</code></li>
    <li><strong>Update + return</strong> — <code>count += 1</code> then <code>return count</code></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The <code>in</code> operator on strings</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`print("a" in "banana")       # True (single char)
print("ana" in "banana")     # True (substring)
print("z" in "banana")       # False`}
  />
</Lecture>
